outcome. Equation. Distinguish conditional vs counterfactual. 350 words.\n3. Causal models operationalization (my addition): structural causal models, variables, do-operator. P(O | do(A)). Then U_CDT(A)=sum_o P(O=o | do(A)) u(o). This gives precise algorithm. For policies, intervention on policy variable or dynamic decisions. This addresses source vagueness. Need mark as my proposal, not source. Maybe mention Lewis imaging as source proposal and relation to possible worlds. 500 words.\n4. Difference from EDT and King David example. Use probabilities: Charisma C common cause of action? Actually David's charisma influences revolt and just acts but action cannot change charisma. EDT abstain because summoning is evidence of uncharismatic? Wait source says EDT recommends abstain; CDT send for her because charisma fixed. Need explain. Maybe define variables: C charisma, A send, R revolt, B benefit? If desire Bathsheba utility positive, revolt negative. C causes A? Charismatic kings act justly; uncharismatic unjustly, so if David sends, evidence uncharismatic, higher revolt. But A doesn't cause C; CDT holds C distribution fixed under do(A): P(C | do(A))=P(C), not P(C|A). If expected utility with do(A) positive, send. 300 words.\n5. Newcomb example: predictor as common cause or prior prediction. CDT two-box because predictor's prediction already fixed; action doesn't causally affect contents (assuming no backward causation). Compute: if B contains M, two-box gets M+1000 vs one-box M; if empty, two-box 1000 vs one-box 0. Under do(two-box), contents distribution unchanged; dominance. EDT one-box due to evidential correlation. 300 words.\n6. Dependencies, counterfactuals, policies: relevant dependencies are causal or counterfactual dependencies from action to outcomes; correlations via common causes are screened off by intervention. Need discuss causal sufficiency, confounding, preemption, overdetermination? Maybe not too much. Explain policies: if action is a rule mapping info to choices, evaluate intervention setting rule? In sequential problems, CDT can choose policy by considering causal effect of adopting policy, but if predictor predicts policy, careful. Need discuss local vs global? Source not explicit. My operational proposal: introduce policy variable Π that determines actions at decision points, intervene do(Π=π), compute outcomes. However, if the agent's choice at a later node is itself an intervention, standard CDT evaluates only current action, which can create dynamic inconsistency? Mention uncertainty. 400 words.\n7. Open questions / vagueness: source says CDT doesn't specify algorithm for counterfactual probabilities; Lewis imaging regress. Need explain. My operational interpretation via SCM requires causal model and prior over models; if uncertain about causal structure, take expectation over dependency hypotheses, holding them fixed under intervention. Need define dependency hypotheses: complete causal graphs/structural equations. U(A)=sum_h P(h) sum_o P_h(o|do(A)) u(o). This is reasonable. But if action provides evidence about hypothesis? CDT says don't conditionalize causal model on action unless action is evidence due to common causes? There is nuance: if actions are correlated with causal structure via self-knowledge? Egan criticism: holding fixed unconditional credences can lead to bad decisions. Need discuss. 400 words.\n8. Counterexamples and limits: Death in Damascus, psychopath button, adversarial offer. Explain why CDT may fail according to critics: predictor correlated with action but not caused by it; CDT treats prediction as fixed. In Death in Damascus, if Death predicts your move, fleeing may not improve causal expected survival; CDT may randomize? Source says unable to process correlation. Psychopath button: pressing button causes psychopaths killed? But only psychopath would press; if psychopathy is common cause of pressing and being killed? Wait Paul prefers living with psychopaths to dying. If pressing