 = .75*3 -1 =1.25, buy. But ex post seller profits? Actually if buyer buys box that seller predicted not buy, gets $3 net +2; if predicted buy, gets 0 net -1. If prediction accurate .75 of buyer's actual choice, buyer loses on average .75* -1 + .25*2 = -0.25. CDT's do uses prior maybe wrong if prediction correlated with action via common cause (buyer's disposition). This illustrates CDT can be exploited. Need maybe include briefly. 450 words.\n9. Practical recipe / how to decide unfamiliar cases: list steps. 250 words.\n10. Conclusion. 100 words.\nReferences: brief source and maybe Pearl? User said avoid lengthy bibliographies. But include references? target includes references. Need cite source and maybe mention Pearl as own addition. Could have short reference list: Wikipedia source, Gibbard & Harper, Lewis, Pearl maybe. But user says prioritize explanation over exhaustive coverage, avoid lengthy bibliographies. Could include minimal references in prose or brief list. Need 2400-3000 words including references. Need be careful word count. Maybe ~2700. We can craft with headings.\n\nNeed ensure final answer is essay, not answer to assigned case. Need maybe mention source: \"The reference article states...\" Distinguish source claims vs own. Need maybe note source's formula: P(A -> O_j) probability of counterfactual. Source says \"necessarily implies\" maybe weird; I can interpret as probability that counterfactual conditional A □→ O_j is true. Need not overdo.\n\nNeed think through formal definitions in detail:\n\nClassical decision problem:\n- finite set of acts \\mathcal A.\n- set of possible worlds W or states S, with probability P.\n- outcome function f: A x S -> O, utility u: O -> R.\nEDT: V_EDT(A)=Σ_s P(S=s | A) u(f(A,s)) or Σ_o P(O=o | A) u(o). But P(S|A) conditional on news A.\nCDT: V_CDT(A)=Σ_s P(A □→ S? or A □→ O?)... Use counterfactual probabilities: P(A □→ O=o) maybe probability that if A were done, O=o. Then V=Σ_o P(A □→ O=o)u(o).\nIn SCM: variables V, equations, exogenous U with distribution. Act A is variable. Intervention do(A=a) replaces equation for A with constant a. Outcome distribution P_{M,a}(O=o). Then V(a)=E_{P_{do(a)}}[u(O)]. For uncertain models M: V(a)=Σ_m P(m) E_{P_{m,do(a)}}[u(O)].\nNeed discuss policy: Suppose policy π maps observations H_t to actions A_t. Introduce policy variable Π that sets decision rule. Intervention do(Π=π) changes structural equations for action variables accordingly. Utility V(π)=E[u(O)|do(Π=π)]. But if policy evaluation asks \"what if I adopted this policy?\" CDT may evaluate adoption as action. However if at each moment agent reconsiders, CDT may not choose policy that requires future actions not locally optimal. This is source? Not in source. Mark as my operational addition and caution.\n\nCounterfactual vs conditional:\nLewis result: probability of conditional not equal conditional probability. If they were equal, CDT=EDT. Gibbard/Harper axioms plus independence of A and A->O_j guarantee equality. But cases actions and conditionals not independent. Need explain independence: If act A is probabilistically independent of the counterfactual \"if A then O\", then learning A doesn't change probability of O under A? Hmm. Source says statistical independence of A and A -> O_j suffices to guarantee equality. Need state but maybe not deeply derive. Could mention: when acts are \"causally independent\" of the truth of counterfactuals, evidential and causal probabilities coincide; otherwise they diverge.\n\nNeed maybe define \"dependency hypotheses\" from Joyce? Source mentions Egan: holding fixed unconditional credences in dependency hypotheses. Could define: a dependency hypothesis h specifies how outcomes depend counterfactually on actions. CDT: V(A)=Σ_h P(h) Σ_o P_h(O=o | do(A)) u(o). It does not update P(h) on A unless A is evidence about h via common cause? Actually in standard CDT, dependency hypotheses are probabilistically independent of actions? Joyce's formulation: causal expected utility uses unconditional probabilities of dependency hypotheses. If agent has news about likely action? Hmm. Need maybe not overcomplicate. But source says Egan argues CDT's requirement to hold fixed unconditional credences in dependency hypotheses leads irrational. Need explain: dependency hypotheses are causal laws/structure; CDT does not let the act itself provide evidence about which causal laws obtain, except if there is a causal route. In psychopath button, pressing is evidence about psychopathy, but psychopathy may be part of dependency hypothesis? If psychopathy is a state affecting both disposition to press and outcome, CDT should hold it fixed? Actually if psychopathy is a state variable not changed by pressing, do(press) should not change distribution of psychopathy. CDT uses prior P(psychopath), not P(psychopath | press). If prior low and benefit high, press. Intuitive says don't press because if you press you're probably psychopath and die. This is EDT-like. Good.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"Newcomb's problem\" source says CDT recommends two boxes because predictor already predicted; action won't affect outcome. But if predictor's prediction is caused by action via backward causation? CDT would differ. Need mention assumptions: no causal influence from current action to past prediction; predictor is merely correlated/common cause. If there is a causal link (e.g., simulation pre-commitment), model changes.\n\nNeed maybe discuss \"imaging\": Lewis proposed moving probability mass to closest A-worlds. Equation: P_im(A□→O)=Σ_w P(w) 1_{O at closest A-world to w} or probabilistic: Σ_w P(w) P_w^A(O). Need mention regress: need beliefs under certainty of A. Source says requires knowing what we would believe if certain of A; conditional to which assign probability <1 leading regress. Need mention that SCM is one modern way to specify imaging via structural equations, but still requires causal assumptions.\n\nNeed ensure not to claim things unsupported if source not mention Pearl. Clearly mark: \"The source article does not supply this algorithm; the following is a natural operationalization I add.\" Good.\n\nNeed maybe include mathematical example King David with variables and table? Could use equations.\n\nLet's design exact essay.\n\nTitle: Causal Decision Theory: A Working Mathematical Guide\n\nParagraphs:\n\nIntro:\n- rational choice under uncertainty; two expected utilities. Source: CDT selects action causing best outcome in expectation; EDT selects action that would be good news. Mention CDT prominent. Need about 150 words.\n\nSection 1: Evidential baseline.\n- Define decision problem: acts a ∈ A; outcomes o ∈ O; utility D(o); probability P over worlds. EDT formula: V_EDT(a)=Σ_o P(o | a)D(o). It treats choosing a as evidence. Example bad apple? maybe brief. Need maybe include apple/orange: eating apple causes happiness if good, sadness if bad. If uncertain, expected causal effects depend on prior good/bad, utilities. This is from source informal. Could include: if P(good)=0.8, D(happy)=10, D(sad)=-5; expected = 0.8*10+0.2*(-5)=7 for apple; if orange certain sad -2, choose apple. But CDT vs EDT difference when correlations. Maybe simple.\n\nSection 2: Causal expected utility with counterfactuals.\n- Gibbard/Harper formula: U(A)=Σ_j P(A→O_j)D(O_j). Need maybe use box arrow □→ to avoid material conditional. P(A □→ O_j) is probability assigned to counterfactual \"if A were performed, O_j would obtain.\" Not conditional P(O_j|A). Lewis proved probabilities of conditionals not generally equal conditional probabilities; if equal CDT=EDT. Mention independence condition. Source says Gibbard/Harper axioms and statistical independence of A and A->O_j sufficient; not always. Need explain in words.\n\nSection 3: Operationalizing with causal models (own proposal).\n- Because source says CDT doesn't specify algorithm. I propose SCM/do-operator. Define variables X, action A, outcome O, structural equations X_i=f_i(PA_i,U_i), exogenous distribution. Intervention do(A=a): replace equation A=f_A(...) with A=a. Then counterfactual probability P(A=a □→ O=o) = P_M(O=o | do(A=a)). Utility: U_CDT(a)=Σ_o P_M(o|do(a))D(o). If uncertain over models M_i with prior q_i: U(a)=Σ_i q_i Σ_o P_{M_i}(o|do(a))D(o). This is a recipe. Discuss policies: variable Π; do(Π=π). For dynamic, local CDT may evaluate immediate action; global policy evaluation requires intervention on policy variable; potential dynamic inconsistency if not bind future self. Need mention source leaves open; this is my interpretation.\n\nNeed maybe include \"screening off confounders\" example: common cause C. If A and O correlated via C, do(A) leaves C distribution unchanged: P(C|do(A))=P(C), not P(C|A). Then U_CDT differs from EDT. Good.\n\nSection 4: King David example.\n- Variables C ∈ {charismatic, uncharismatic}; A ∈ {send, abstain}; R revolt; utility. C fixed prior p. C causes just/unjust acts and revolt risk; A may be evidence of C but doesn't cause C. EDT uses P(C|A=send) high uncharismatic; may abstain. CDT uses P(C|do(send))=P(C); if sending has direct benefit and no causal effect on revolt except maybe through unjust act? Wait source says unjust acts themselves do not cause revolts; uncharismatic kings prone due bearing. So A=send is unjust but doesn't cause revolt; C causes both. Therefore CDT sees no causal path from send to revolt except maybe if sending causes something? It says sending for Bathsheba is unjust, but unjust acts don't cause revolts. So expected utility: U(send)=p*U(send,C)+... maybe direct pleasure minus no revolt change? If desire outweighs, send. Need formulate: If P(C)=prior, U(send)-U(abstain)=desire plus maybe no causal change in revolt. So CDT sends. Good.\n\nSection 5: Newcomb.\n- Variables P (prediction), B contents, choice T (one/two). Predictor sets B based on P. Causal assumption: choice occurs after content set, no backward causation. do(T=two) does not change B. Outcomes: if B=M, two M+1000 > one M; if B=0, two 1000 > one 0. Thus two-box dominates; U(two)=P(B=M)* (M+1000)+P(B=0)*1000; U(one)=P(B=M)*M + P(B=0)*0; difference=1000. EDT: P(B=M|one)≈.99, P(B=M|two)≈.01; one-box if .99M > .01M+1000? Yes. Mention source. CDT's answer hinges on causal model; if choice causally influences prediction (e.g., via predictor simulating decision process at time of choice), model differs. Source says at moment predictor already predicted; action won't affect outcome.\n\nSection 6: Dependencies and counterfactuals.\n- CDT evaluates relevant dependencies as counterfactual dependence under intervention, not mere statistical dependence. If O counterfactually depends on A through causal path, included. If A is evidence for O via common cause, not included unless intervention changes common cause? Need be precise: intervention on A may change descendants of A, not ancestors/common causes. Discuss confounding. In applying, ask: which variables would change if action were imposed by external manipulation? This is do. Need mention uncertainty about causal structure: average over dependency hypotheses; actions don't update dependency hypotheses unless action is evidence about them through a causal common cause? Hmm. Could say: If an action is correlated with a causal hypothesis because agents with certain dispositions choose it, CDT's standard formulation still holds the prior over hypotheses fixed when computing the act's causal value; this is the source of many objections. Need mark.\n\nSection 7: Vagueness, imaging, regress.\n- Source: CDT does not specify algorithm to calculate counterfactual probabilities. Lewis imaging: for each world w, move mass to closest A-world w_A; P(A□→O)=Σ_w P(w) I[w_A∈O]. Requires closest-world similarity and beliefs if certain of A; regress. SCM can be seen as specifying similarity/counterfactuals via equations, but needs causal assumptions. Source leaves open; my proposal: use explicit causal model or distribution over models. If no model, CDT