 or likely to do as evidence. This is a defense. Could mention as open dispute.\n- Adversarial Offer: explain as money extraction. Use equations. Buyer can buy one or none. Seller predicts not acquire, puts $3. Prediction accuracy .75. CDT uses do(buy box) with prior probability box contains $3 maybe .75; expected value .75*3 -1 = 1.25 >0, buys. But if prediction tracks actual choice, ex post expected profit = .25*(3-1)+.75*(0-1)= -0.25, seller profits. This illustrates if action is correlated with predictor due to common disposition, CDT's holding fixed can be exploitable. Source says CDT requires buying one; profitable for seller. Need maybe mention Oesterheld/Conitzer Dutch books.\n\nSection 9: Practical recipe.\n- Steps: 1 specify acts/outcomes/utilities; 2 draw causal graph or dependency hypotheses; 3 identify action variable; 4 intervene (do), not condition; 5 compute outcome distribution; 6 take expectation; 7 for policies intervene on policy variable; 8 check dynamic consistency/precommitment; 9 if causal model uncertain, average over models with prior; 10 note if only correlations, CDT may ignore them. This helps unfamiliar cases.\n\nConclusion.\n\nNeed word count. Let's estimate: intro 150, baseline 250, counterfactual 300, operationalization 500, examples 600, dependencies/open 500, counterexamples 500, recipe 250, conclusion 100, references 100 = 3250. Need trim to under 3000. Maybe target 2700. Need concise. Could combine sections. Let's write with around 2600-2800. Need count approximate. Use headings. Ensure references not lengthy. Maybe in-text citations: (Wikipedia source), Gibbard & Harper, Lewis, Pearl? Since source asks target including references. Could include a short \"References\" list with 4 items. Word count includes references. Keep references ~80 words. Need maybe not include full bibliographic but enough. Source article, Gibbard & Harper, Lewis, Pearl maybe. But Pearl not in source; if mention as own addition, include? Could include \"Pearl 2009\" but avoid lengthy. Maybe references: Wikipedia revision; Gibbard & Harper 1981; Lewis 1981; Pearl 2009. Need maybe source references? Could just cite source article and two key. User said avoid lengthy bibliographies. We'll have brief list.\n\nNeed make clear source claims vs my additions. Use phrases: \"The reference article states...\", \"The source leaves open...\", \"I will add...\", \"This operationalization is not in the source but is a natural mathematical implementation.\"\n\nNeed avoid giving unsupported claim \"CDT requires buying one\" source says. Good.\n\nNeed consider exact formalization of Gibbard-Harper formula: P(A → O_j) D(O_j). In source says probability of counterfactual of it being the case that A necessarily implies O_j. Maybe use notation P(A \\boxright O_j). Need define: probability that counterfactual \"if A were performed, O_j would obtain\" is true. If outcomes partition. Then U(A). Need maybe note if probabilistic outcomes, the counterfactual may be chance; P(A□→O) can be interpreted as chance or credence in chance. Good.\n\nNeed discuss \"counterfactual probability\" vs conditional probability. Example: P(heads | toss) vs P(if tossed heads). In deterministic? Maybe not necessary.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"principle of conditional excluded middle\"? Source says Gibbard/Harper axioms one related to controversial principle. Could mention in passing: under certain logical axioms and independence, equality. But not dwell.\n\nNeed maybe discuss \"statistical independence of A and A→O_j\". We can write: If the event that the agent performs A is independent of the truth of the counterfactual A□→O_j, then learning A doesn't alter probability of O_j under A, so P(A□→O_j)=P(O_j|A). But in King David, performing A is evidence about charisma which affects the counterfactual? Hmm. Need maybe not derive. Good.\n\nPotential issue: In SCM, P(O|do(A)) is not exactly probability of counterfactual A□→O if there is nondeterminism? It's probability of outcome under intervention, yes. Counterfactuals in SCM can be defined with exogenous variables; but for decision before exogenous observed, expected utility uses interventional distribution. Good.\n\nNeed maybe discuss \"policies\" more. The user specifically asks: \"Explain how the theory evaluates alternative actions or policies and how it treats relevant dependencies and counterfactuals.\" Need ensure policies. Could say: For a policy π, introduce policy variable and evaluate do(Π=π); if policy maps information to actions, intervention sets all action nodes according to π. However, source mainly speaks of actions; policy evaluation is an extension. If the agent will later choose under CDT, a policy may not be credible unless precommitted. CDT can evaluate precommitment device as an action that changes future causal structure. This is important. Need include.\n\nNeed maybe include \"decision problems with unknown causal structure\". Let's formulate:\nLet H be set of dependency hypotheses. Each h specifies a function or distribution P_h(o | do(a)). Agent has prior p(h). Then CDT value:\nV_CDT(a)=∑_h p(h) ∑_o P_h(o | do(a)) D(o).\nThis is source-inspired (Egan mentions dependency hypotheses) and my operationalization. It holds p(h) fixed, not p(h|a). If action is evidence about h via common cause, EDT would use p(h|a). CDT says no unless causal. This is central.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"if action itself changes evidence about state?\" In CDT, if action causes state, then do includes that causal link. If action is merely evidence, no. Good.\n\nNeed maybe discuss \"news value\" vs causal value: EDT can choose action for good news; CDT rejects managing news. Example: If smoking is evidence of relaxation but doesn't cause cancer? Actually classic smoking lesion. Could mention but not needed. Maybe King David enough.\n\nNeed maybe use equations for Newcomb:\nLet p = P(B=M). CDT values:\nV(two)=p(M+1000)+(1-p)1000 = 1000 + pM.\nV(one)=pM+(1-p)0=pM.\nDifference 1000. Regardless of predictor accuracy p? Wait p is prior under do? If predictor accuracy, p maybe prior probability predicted one-box. Under do(two), contents distribution might equal prior probability predictor predicted one-box, not conditional on actual choice. If prior over predictor's prediction is maybe 0.5. Then difference 1000. Good.\nEDT values with accuracy .99:\nV_EDT(one)≈0.99M+0.01*0; V_EDT(two)≈0.01(M+1000)+0.99*1000. For M=1,000,000, one much larger. Good.\n\nNeed maybe use equations for adversarial offer:\nLet accuracy q=0.75. If CDT assigns P($3 in box i | do(buy i))=0.75? Actually source says CDT reasons at least one box contains $3; average box at least $1.50. How derive? If seller accurate .75, from buyer's perspective before choice, expected number of filled boxes = 2*0.75? Wait each box predicted not acquired with probability? Hmm. Source: \"CDT reasons that at least one box contains $3. Therefore, average box contains at least $1.50 in causal expected value.\" Maybe because seller accurate .75: if buyer chooses one box, the other box is not acquired, so if seller predicted correctly about other, it contains $3 with .75? Actually buyer may purchase one or none but not both. If buyer buys one, the other is not acquired. Seller predicted for each box whether buyer will acquire. Since she is accurate .75, at least one of the two predictions? Let's parse: Two boxes. Buyer can buy one or none, not both. Seller put $3 in each box she predicted buyer not to acquire. Prediction accurate with prob .75. From buyer's perspective, if they buy exactly one, for the unchosen box, seller's prediction \"not acquire\" is likely correct (prob .75), so unchosen box likely contains $3. But the chosen box? Seller predicted acquire with .75, so empty. CDT evaluating buying a box maybe sees causal effect of buying chosen box doesn't affect whether unchosen has money? Wait source says \"CDT reasons that at least one box contains $3. Therefore, the average box contains at least