## 4. Examples: David, Newcomb\nMaybe combine.\n\n## 5. What dependencies count?\nCommon causes, confounders, policy, dynamic.\n\n## 6. Open problems and counterexamples\nImaging regress, Death, psychopath, adversarial offer.\n\n## 7. Practical checklist\nSteps.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nReferences.\n\nNeed word count. Let's estimate each section:\nIntro 120\nTwo EU 260\nCounterfactual 260\nOperationalization 480\nExamples 500\nDependencies 300\nOpen/counterexamples 550\nChecklist 200\nConclusion 80\nRefs 100\nTotal ~2850. Good.\n\nNeed include mathematical definitions cleanly.\n\nLet's write final carefully. Need maybe use LaTeX notation. Need avoid overlong references. Need maybe cite source: \"The reference article summarizes CDT as...\" Good.\n\nPotential nuance: Source formula uses P(A → O_j) where arrow is counterfactual. In LaTeX maybe \\(P(A \\mathbin{\\square\\!\\to} O_j)\\). Could use \\(P(A \\counterfactual O_j)\\) but standard is \\(A \\boxright O\\). Use \\(A \\mathbin{\\triangleright} O\\)? Better: \\(P(A \\mathbin{\\square\\rightarrow} O_j)\\). Explain.\n\nNeed ensure mathematical correctness: In SCM, if outcomes are functions of exogenous variables, counterfactual probability after observing evidence can be more complex. For choice ex ante, interventional distribution is enough. Could mention \"for an agent who has not yet acted, the decision-relevant counterfactual probability is the interventional probability.\" Good.\n\nNeed mention \"counterfactual probability of it being the case that A necessarily implies O_j\" maybe source says necessarily. I can write \"probability that the counterfactual conditional 'if A were done, O_j would result' is true.\" Good.\n\nNeed discuss \"if conditional probabilities equal counterfactual probabilities\". Equation: If \\(P(A \\square\\rightarrow O)=P(O|A)\\) for all O, CDT=EDT. Lewis showed not generally. Gibbard/Harper: under axioms and independence, equality. Good.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"principle of conditional excluded middle\" not important but source says. Could say: \"with additional logical axioms, including a controversial conditional excluded middle\". Good.\n\nNeed maybe include \"dependency hypothesis\" formula:\nLet \\(H\\) denote a causal dependency hypothesis. Then\n\\[V_{\\mathrm{CDT}}(a)=\\sum_h P(H=h)\\sum_o P_h(O=o\\mid do(a))D(o).\\]\nThe source's discussion of Egan suggests CDT holds \\(P(H)\\) fixed rather than using \\(P(H|a)\\). Need state as my formalization. Good.\n\nNeed maybe include \"policy\" formula:\n\\[V_{\\mathrm{CDT}}(\\pi)=\\sum_o P(O=o\\mid do(\\Pi=\\pi))D(o).\\]\nNeed define \\(\\Pi\\). Good.\n\nNeed maybe include \"local action\" formula: At each decision node, choose action maximizing immediate causal EU given current info, but if policy requires future non-myopic choices, precommitment. Good.\n\nPotential issue: In sequential decision problems, standard CDT can evaluate policies by intervention on policy variable, but some philosophers debate whether CDT is dynamic inconsistent. Need mention source leaves open; my operational interpretation. Good.\n\nNeed maybe discuss \"news\" and \"management\". Good.\n\nLet's think of possible edge cases: If action has zero prior probability, conditional probability undefined; CDT can still evaluate via counterfactuals. This is a benefit. Could mention? Useful. In EDT, P(O|A) may be undefined or unstable for acts agent believes impossible; CDT with interventions can evaluate. Maybe include in formal section. Not necessary but good.\n\nNeed maybe discuss \"causal decision theory recommends actions causing best expected outcome\" but if action itself changes outcome only through evidence? Example: smiling makes people like you? That's causal. Good.\n\nNeed maybe discuss \"holding fixed initial views about likely causal structure\" from Egan. Use quote-like. Good.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"source says philosophers usually prefer CDT when required to choose between CDT and EDT.\" Could include. But not necessary. Could mention to contextualize.\n\nNeed maybe include \"apple/orange\" informal. Use in first section. Let's craft:\n\"If eating an apple causes happiness and orange causes sadness, choose apple. If apple may be good/bad, expected causal effect = P(good)D(happy)+P(bad)D(sad).\" This from source. Good.\n\nNeed maybe include exact numbers? Could keep simple. Maybe no numbers to save words. But examples help. Use small numbers maybe. \"If P(good)=0.8, happy=10, sad=-5, expected apple=7; if orange=-2, choose apple.\" Good but word count. Fine.\n\nNeed maybe note \"desirability D\" not probability; utility.\n\nNeed think about references and licensing. Since source is Wikipedia CC BY-SA, if we adapt maybe need attribution. The user supplied source; we can include reference. We can state \"This essay draws on the Wikipedia article...\" Good.\n\nNeed final conclusion brief.\n\nNeed ensure no analysis in final. Final only essay.\n\nLet's begin writing final. Need keep word count. I'll produce around 2700 words. I can roughly count? Let's write and approximate. Maybe use word counter mentally? We'll try.\n\nDraft:\n\n# Causal Decision Theory: Choosing by Expected Causal Consequences\n\nIntro: 130\n\n## 1. Expected utility, evidentially understood\n...\n\nLet's draft fully:\n\n[Will write final in my head then output.]\n\nNeed be careful with LaTeX syntax. Use \\( ... \\). Good.\n\nPotential final text:\n\n\"Causal decision theory (CDT) is a school of thought within decision theory holding that a rational agent should choose the action that causes the best outcome in expectation. The reference article contrasts this with evidential decision theory (EDT), which chooses the action that would be the best news if the agent learned it had been taken.\" Good.\n\nThen formal.\n\nNeed maybe include \"The source article's central formula is...\" Good.\n\nLet's write section 1:\n\n\"Let \\(\\mathcal A\\) be a set of available acts, \\(\\mathcal O\\) outcomes, and \\(D:\\mathcal O\\to\\mathbb R\\) desirability. A probability function \\(P\\) represents the agent's credences. EDT evaluates\n\\[V_{EDT}(a)=\\sum_o P(O=o\\mid A=a)D(o).\\]\nThis is conditional expectation: it asks what outcome should be expected given the news that \\(a\\) was performed. If eating an apple is likely to cause happiness... But conditional probabilities can encode merely evidential connections.\" Good.\n\nSection 2:\n\n\"Gibbard and Harper... \\(U(A)=\\sum_j P(A \\square\\rightarrow O_j)D(O_j)\\). The symbol ... probability of counterfactual. If outcomes exhaustive, etc. Lewis proved ... not equal. If equal, CDT collapses to EDT. Gibbard/Harper showed under axioms + independence equality; but actions/conditionals not independent in cases like King David.\" Good.\n\nNeed maybe define \\(A\\) as proposition event? Fine.\n\nSection 3 operationalization:\n\n\"The source says CDT does not itself specify an algorithm for computing \\(P(A\\square\\rightarrow O)\\). I will therefore add a standard modern operationalization...\" Good.\n\nDefine SCM:\n\"A structural causal model \\(M\\) has variables \\(V\\), equations \\(X_i=f_i(PA_i,U_i)\\), exogenous distribution. For action variable \\(A\\), intervention \\(do(A=a)\\) replaces equation for A. Then \\(P_M(O=o\\mid do(A=a))\\) is the probability that outcome would occur if A were imposed. CDT value ... If multiple models h with prior p(h), ...\" Good.\n\nPolicy:\n\"To evaluate policy \\(\\pi\\), introduce policy variable \\(\\Pi\\) and set \\(do(\\Pi=\\pi)\\), changing all action equations according to π. This is my extension; source focuses on single acts. In sequential settings, evaluating adoption of policy may differ from evaluating each future act locally; precommitment can change causal structure.\" Good.\n\nSection 4 examples:\n\nKing David:\n\"David wants Bathsheba; fears revolt. Charisma C fixed. C causes revolt risk and just/unjust acts; sending is unjust but doesn't cause revolt. EDT: P(C=uncharismatic | send) high, so revolt expected, abstain. CDT: do(send) leaves distribution of C unchanged; no causal path from send to revolt; if direct utility of Bathsheba positive, send. Mathematically, \\(P(C\\mid do(send))=P(C)\\), whereas EDT uses \\(P(C\\mid send)\\).\" Good.\n\nNewcomb:\n\"Predictor sets box B before choice. Let B=M or 0. CDT assumes no causal arrow from choice to B. do(two) leaves B distribution fixed. Values... difference 1000; two-box. EDT uses conditional probabilities, one-box.\" Good.\n\nSection 5 dependencies:\n\n\"CDT counts dependencies that survive intervention. If O changes when A is externally set, relevant. Correlations due to common cause C are not relevant unless A causally influences C or C is downstream. The practical question: Which variables would change if the act were imposed? In dependency hypothesis notation... CDT uses prior over hypotheses. This is what Egan criticizes: holding fixed unconditional credences.\" Good.\n\nSection 6 vagueness/counterexamples:\n\n\"Source notes vagueness; Lewis imaging... regress. SCM addresses but requires causal model. Counterexamples: Death in Damascus, psychopath button, adversarial offer.\" Need detail.\n\nDeath: \"Death predicts location; if reliable, your choice is correlated with prediction. CDT may not see fleeing as causally improving because Death's prediction already fixed; critics say this is irrational.\" Maybe mention if Death prediction is perfect, every act has bad conditional evidence; CDT may be indifferent or randomize, but source says unable to process correlation. Good.\n\nPsychopath: \"Paul... CDT may press if prior psychopath low, because pressing causes killing psychopaths only if not psychopath; but pressing is evidence he is psychopath. Intuitive not press. CDT's holding fixed psychopathy yields press according to Egan.\" Need be precise: If button kills all psychopaths, and Paul may be one, causal effect of pressing includes death if psychopath. Under do(press), psychopathy fixed. Expected utility = P(psychopath)*D(death)+P(not)*D(no psychopaths). If P(psychopath) very small and D(no psychopaths) high, press. EDT uses P(psychopath|press) high, not press. Good.\n\nAdversarial offer: \"Two boxes cost $1; seller put $3 in boxes predicted not acquired; accuracy .75. Source says CDT reasons at least one contains $3, average box ≥$1.50, so buy. But if prediction tracks buyer's choice, buying tends to select empty box; seller profits. This supports claim CDT can voluntarily lose money and be Dutch-booked.\" Good.\n\nDefender response: \"Joyce says CDT can take information about inclinations as evidence; disputes remain. Source leaves whether these are fatal open.\" Good.\n\nSection 7 checklist:\n\n\"For unfamiliar case: 1 list actions/outcomes/utility; 2 specify causal graph/dependency hypotheses; 3 identify action variable; 4 replace conditioning with intervention; 5 compute distribution; 6 average over uncertainty; 7 for policies intervene on policy variable; 8 check if act provides only news; 9 consider precommitment/dynamic consistency.\" Good.\n\nConclusion.\n\nReferences:\n- Wikipedia contributors, Causal decision theory, revision 1381555528, 2026.\n- Gibbard & Harper 1981.\n- Lewis 1981 maybe 1976? Need mention Lewis 1976 probabilities conditionals. Could include both? Keep short: Lewis 1976; Lewis 1981. Maybe 3 refs plus Pearl. But lengthy? We'll list 4.\n\nNeed maybe not include full details but enough.\n\nNow, need ensure final word count. Let's approximate after writing. We'll output. But maybe need more mathematical precision in policy and dependencies. Good.\n\nLet's write final now with polished prose. Ensure approx 2400-3000. I can maybe include around 2500. Need avoid too many examples? The user asks use examples whenever helpful. Good.\n\nLet's draft final fully.\n\nPotential final:\n\n(Start)\n\n# Causal Decision Theory: Expected Utility as Causal Expectation\n\n... etc.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"source article says CDT contrasts EDT\". Good.\n\nLet's produce final. Ensure no meta. Ensure references. Need maybe use \"I\" for own proposals? It's an essay, okay. Use \"I add\". Good.\n\nDouble-check word count roughly. I can count approximate by section lengths.\n\nI'll write with moderate length. If too short, add details. Let's estimate while writing:\n\nIntro 130\nSection1 300\nSection2 300\nSection3 550\nSection4 450\nSection5 300\nSection6 550\nSection7 200\nConclusion 80\nRefs 100 = 2960. Good.\n\nNeed maybe not exceed 3000. Fine.\n\nLet's write.\n\nOne subtlety: Source formula: \\(P(A \\to O_j)\\) probability of counterfactual that A necessarily implies O_j. If using \\(\\square\\to\\), okay. Need maybe note \"I write \\(\\square\\to\\) to avoid confusion with material implication.\" Good.\n\nAnother subtlety: In SCM formula for uncertain models, if action is evidence about model h due to common cause, CDT holds P(h) fixed. But if the agent has observed evidence E, prior should be conditioned on E before decision. Yes: \"conditional on all information available before choice, but not on the act itself.\" Need include. In recipe: update on background evidence, then intervene. Good.\n\nNeed mention \"when act has zero prior probability\" maybe in section 2: EDT may struggle; CDT counterfactual can evaluate. Good.\n\nNeed mention \"dependency hypotheses\" perhaps in section 5: define. Good.\n\nPotential final text includes equations:\n\\[V_{\\mathrm{CDT}}(a)=\\sum_o P(a\\square\\to O=o)D(o).\\]\n\\[V_{\\mathrm{EDT}}(a)=\\sum_o P(O=o\\mid A=a)D(o).\\]\n\\[P_M(O=o\\mid do(A=a))\\]\n\\[V_{\\mathrm{CDT}}(a)=\\sum_h p(h)\\sum_o P_{h}(O=o\\mid do(A=a))D(o).\\]\n\\[V_{\\mathrm{CDT}}(\\pi)=\\sum_o P(O=o\\mid do(\\Pi=\\pi))D(o).\\]\nNewcomb values. Good.\n\nNeed perhaps mention \"causal dominance\": if for every state fixed by intervention, a yields better than b, choose a. In Newcomb, two-box causally dominates given contents fixed. Good.\n\nLet's final. Ensure no citations bracket too many. Use parenthetical names. References short.\n\nOne concern: The source says \"probability of counterfactuals\" not necessarily interventional probability; in possible-world semantics, probability of counterfactual may differ from interventional distribution if antecedent action is correlated with background? But in decision theory, standard. Good.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"Lewis imaging\" equation:\n\\[P_{\\text{im}}(a\\square\\to O)=\\sum_w P(w) \\mathbf{1}_{O}(w_a),\\]\nwhere \\(w_a\\) is closest a-world. Good. Adds mathematical detail. Good.\n\nNeed maybe mention regress: \"to know what mass to move, need probability conditional on being certain of a\". Good.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"conditional excluded middle\" maybe not necessary. But source says. Could add one sentence: \"Gibbard and Harper's equality result relies on additional logical assumptions, including a controversial principle related to conditional excluded middle.\" Good.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"philosophers usually prefer CDT\" from source? Could include intro or conclusion: \"When forced to choose, many philosophers prefer CDT, but counterexamples persist.\" Good.\n\nLet's produce final. Use clear headings. Ensure target word count. final.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"We need answer user's request: write a mathematical essay/blog post about CDT, applying to decision problems, approx 2400-3000 words including references. Need read source, produce coherent account, definitions, equations, examples, explain evaluation of actions/policies, dependencies/counterfactuals, open questions, operational interpretation if available, distinguish source claims from own assumptions. Finish brief conclusion. Need likely produce final essay only. Need ensure word count roughly 2400-3000. Need maybe include references brief. Need avoid unnecessary repetition, lengthy bibliographies, appendices. Need target around 2600 words maybe. Need produce substantive readable mathematical essay. Need include formal definitions: decision problem, states, actions, outcomes, utility, EDT vs CDT, counterfactual probabilities, causal models (SCMs) perhaps my own addition to operationalize. Source says CDT doesn't specify algorithm; we can develop operational interpretation using Pearl's do-operator / structural causal models, as my proposal beyond source. Need distinguish source claims from my additions. Need mention Gibbard & Harper formula U(A)=sum_j P(A -> O_j) D(O_j). Lewis: probability of conditional not equal conditional probability. Imaging. Independence conditions. Examples: apple/orange, King David, Newcomb, Death in Damascus maybe, psychopath button, adversarial offer? Need choose examples. Need explain dependencies and counterfactuals. Need discuss policies maybe: CDT evaluates actions at choice point; policies can be evaluated by interventions on policy variables or sequence of actions? Need include how to apply to unfamiliar cases: steps: specify causal model, identify action variable, intervention, compute distribution of outcomes under intervention, expected utility; separate evidence from causal effects. Discuss when causal model unavailable, use counterfactual probabilities or imaging; uncertainty. Need discuss relevant dependencies: common causes, confounders; CDT holds fixed causal structure and unconditional credences in dependency hypotheses? Source Egan criticism. Need maybe discuss ratifiability? Not necessary but could mention. Need word count. Let's plan essay structure:\n\nTitle: Causal Decision Theory: Choosing by Expected Causal Consequences\n\n1. Introduction: decision theory, CDT vs EDT. approx 200 words.\n2. Basic formal setup: finite acts A, states S, outcomes O, utility D. Expected utility. Evidential EU: U_EDT(A)=sum O P(O|A)D(O). Causal EU: U_CDT(A)=sum O P(A □→ O)D(O) or P(A -> O). Need clarify counterfactual probability: probability that if A were performed, O would occur. Source says P(A -> O_j) probability of counterfactual \"A necessarily implies O_j\"? The formula maybe weird. Need present as probability of counterfactual outcome. Equation. Distinguish conditional vs counterfactual. 350 words.\n3. Causal models operationalization (my addition): structural causal models, variables, do-operator. P(O | do(A)). Then U_CDT(A)=sum_o P(O=o | do(A)) u(o). This gives precise algorithm. For policies, intervention on policy variable or dynamic decisions. This addresses source vagueness. Need mark as my proposal, not source. Maybe mention Lewis imaging as source proposal and relation to possible worlds. 500 words.\n4. Difference from EDT and King David example. Use probabilities: Charisma C common cause of action? Actually David's charisma influences revolt and just acts but action cannot change charisma. EDT abstain because summoning is evidence of uncharismatic? Wait source says EDT recommends abstain; CDT send for her because charisma fixed. Need explain. Maybe define variables: C charisma, A send, R revolt, B benefit? If desire Bathsheba utility positive, revolt negative. C causes A? Charismatic kings act justly; uncharismatic unjustly, so if David sends, evidence uncharismatic, higher revolt. But A doesn't cause C; CDT holds C distribution fixed under do(A): P(C | do(A))=P(C), not P(C|A). If expected utility with do(A) positive, send. 300 words.\n5. Newcomb example: predictor as common cause or prior prediction. CDT two-box because predictor's prediction already fixed; action doesn't causally affect contents (assuming no backward causation). Compute: if B contains M, two-box gets M+1000 vs one-box M; if empty, two-box 1000 vs one-box 0. Under do(two-box), contents distribution unchanged; dominance. EDT one-box due to evidential correlation. 300 words.\n6. Dependencies, counterfactuals, policies: relevant dependencies are causal or counterfactual dependencies from action to outcomes; correlations via common causes are screened off by intervention. Need discuss causal sufficiency, confounding, preemption, overdetermination? Maybe not too much. Explain policies: if action is a rule mapping info to choices, evaluate intervention setting rule? In sequential problems, CDT can choose policy by considering causal effect of adopting policy, but if predictor predicts policy, careful. Need discuss local vs global? Source not explicit. My operational proposal: introduce policy variable Π that determines actions at decision points, intervene do(Π=π), compute outcomes. However, if the agent's choice at a later node is itself an intervention, standard CDT evaluates only current action, which can create dynamic inconsistency? Mention uncertainty. 400 words.\n7. Open questions / vagueness: source says CDT doesn't specify algorithm for counterfactual probabilities; Lewis imaging regress. Need explain. My operational interpretation via SCM requires causal model and prior over models; if uncertain about causal structure, take expectation over dependency hypotheses, holding them fixed under intervention. Need define dependency hypotheses: complete causal graphs/structural equations. U(A)=sum_h P(h) sum_o P_h(o|do(A)) u(o). This is reasonable. But if action provides evidence about hypothesis? CDT says don't conditionalize causal model on action unless action is evidence due to common causes? There is nuance: if actions are correlated with causal structure via self-knowledge? Egan criticism: holding fixed unconditional credences can lead to bad decisions. Need discuss. 400 words.\n8. Counterexamples and limits: Death in Damascus, psychopath button, adversarial offer. Explain why CDT may fail according to critics: predictor correlated with action but not caused by it; CDT treats prediction as fixed. In Death in Damascus, if Death predicts your move, fleeing may not improve causal expected survival; CDT may randomize? Source says unable to process correlation. Psychopath button: pressing button causes psychopaths killed? But only psychopath would press; if psychopathy is common cause of pressing and being killed? Wait Paul prefers living with psychopaths to dying. If pressing kills all psychopaths, if Paul is psychopath he dies; if not, world better. CDT might say pressing causes no-psychopaths if not psychopath? Need parse: Paul confident only psychopath would press. If he presses, this is evidence he is psychopath; but CDT holding fixed psychopathy probability? If button actually kills psychopaths including Paul if psychopath. If Paul not psychopath, pressing causes better world. If prior probability psychopath low, CDT may press because causal effect of pressing conditional on not psychopath? Egan says CDT endorses pressing because it holds fixed initial credence he's psychopath; if he strongly prefers living with psychopaths to dying, intuitive not press because pressing evidences psychopathy. Need explain as conflict. Adversarial offer: source summary: seller predicts buyer not acquire boxes, puts $3 in predicted not acquired. Buyer can buy one or none cost $1 each not both. CDT reasons at least one contains $3? Let's analyze: Two boxes, seller predicts each individually? \"Yesterday, seller put $3 in each box that she predicted the buyer not to acquire. Both believe prediction accurate with prob .75.\" Buyer may purchase one or none but not both. CDT reasons at least one box contains $3? If choose a box, the seller likely predicted buyer not acquire that box? Wait if buyer chooses box i, seller's prediction about i likely predicted not acquire? Accuracy .75: If buyer acquires i, with .75 seller predicted acquire, so no $3. CDT however maybe says for each box, causal expected value > cost because the choice doesn't affect yesterday's money? Let's compute: From buyer perspective before choice, each box has probability .75 of containing $3? If prediction accurate .75, and perhaps buyer's future choice correlated with prediction. If buyer chooses a specific box, conditional on choosing it, likely seller predicted choose, so empty; but CDT holds prediction fixed under do(buy i): P(box i contains $3 | do(buy i)) maybe prior .75? Then EV = .75*3 -1 =1.25, buy. But ex post seller profits? Actually if buyer buys box that seller predicted not buy, gets $3 net +2; if predicted buy, gets 0 net -1. If prediction accurate .75 of buyer's actual choice, buyer loses on average .75* -1 + .25*2 = -0.25. CDT's do uses prior maybe wrong if prediction correlated with action via common cause (buyer's disposition). This illustrates CDT can be exploited. Need maybe include briefly. 450 words.\n9. Practical recipe / how to decide unfamiliar cases: list steps. 250 words.\n10. Conclusion. 100 words.\nReferences: brief source and maybe Pearl? User said avoid lengthy bibliographies. But include references? target includes references. Need cite source and maybe mention Pearl as own addition. Could have short reference list: Wikipedia source, Gibbard & Harper, Lewis, Pearl maybe. But user says prioritize explanation over exhaustive coverage, avoid lengthy bibliographies. Could include minimal references in prose or brief list. Need 2400-3000 words including references. Need be careful word count. Maybe ~2700. We can craft with headings.\n\nNeed ensure final answer is essay, not answer to assigned case. Need maybe mention source: \"The reference article states...\" Distinguish source claims vs own. Need maybe note source's formula: P(A -> O_j) probability of counterfactual. Source says \"necessarily implies\" maybe weird; I can interpret as probability that counterfactual conditional A □→ O_j is true. Need not overdo.\n\nNeed think through formal definitions in detail:\n\nClassical decision problem:\n- finite set of acts \\mathcal A.\n- set of possible worlds W or states S, with probability P.\n- outcome function f: A x S -> O, utility u: O -> R.\nEDT: V_EDT(A)=Σ_s P(S=s | A) u(f(A,s)) or Σ_o P(O=o | A) u(o). But P(S|A) conditional on news A.\nCDT: V_CDT(A)=Σ_s P(A □→ S? or A □→ O?)... Use counterfactual probabilities: P(A □→ O=o) maybe probability that if A were done, O=o. Then V=Σ_o P(A □→ O=o)u(o).\nIn SCM: variables V, equations, exogenous U with distribution. Act A is variable. Intervention do(A=a) replaces equation for A with constant a. Outcome distribution P_{M,a}(O=o). Then V(a)=E_{P_{do(a)}}[u(O)]. For uncertain models M: V(a)=Σ_m P(m) E_{P_{m,do(a)}}[u(O)].\nNeed discuss policy: Suppose policy π maps observations H_t to actions A_t. Introduce policy variable Π that sets decision rule. Intervention do(Π=π) changes structural equations for action variables accordingly. Utility V(π)=E[u(O)|do(Π=π)]. But if policy evaluation asks \"what if I adopted this policy?\" CDT may evaluate adoption as action. However if at each moment agent reconsiders, CDT may not choose policy that requires future actions not locally optimal. This is source? Not in source. Mark as my operational addition and caution.\n\nCounterfactual vs conditional:\nLewis result: probability of conditional not equal conditional probability. If they were equal, CDT=EDT. Gibbard/Harper axioms plus independence of A and A->O_j guarantee equality. But cases actions and conditionals not independent. Need explain independence: If act A is probabilistically independent of the counterfactual \"if A then O\", then learning A doesn't change probability of O under A? Hmm. Source says statistical independence of A and A -> O_j suffices to guarantee equality. Need state but maybe not deeply derive. Could mention: when acts are \"causally independent\" of the truth of counterfactuals, evidential and causal probabilities coincide; otherwise they diverge.\n\nNeed maybe define \"dependency hypotheses\" from Joyce? Source mentions Egan: holding fixed unconditional credences in dependency hypotheses. Could define: a dependency hypothesis h specifies how outcomes depend counterfactually on actions. CDT: V(A)=Σ_h P(h) Σ_o P_h(O=o | do(A)) u(o). It does not update P(h) on A unless A is evidence about h via common cause? Actually in standard CDT, dependency hypotheses are probabilistically independent of actions? Joyce's formulation: causal expected utility uses unconditional probabilities of dependency hypotheses. If agent has news about likely action? Hmm. Need maybe not overcomplicate. But source says Egan argues CDT's requirement to hold fixed unconditional credences in dependency hypotheses leads irrational. Need explain: dependency hypotheses are causal laws/structure; CDT does not let the act itself provide evidence about which causal laws obtain, except if there is a causal route. In psychopath button, pressing is evidence about psychopathy, but psychopathy may be part of dependency hypothesis? If psychopathy is a state affecting both disposition to press and outcome, CDT should hold it fixed? Actually if psychopathy is a state variable not changed by pressing, do(press) should not change distribution of psychopathy. CDT uses prior P(psychopath), not P(psychopath | press). If prior low and benefit high, press. Intuitive says don't press because if you press you're probably psychopath and die. This is EDT-like. Good.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"Newcomb's problem\" source says CDT recommends two boxes because predictor already predicted; action won't affect outcome. But if predictor's prediction is caused by action via backward causation? CDT would differ. Need mention assumptions: no causal influence from current action to past prediction; predictor is merely correlated/common cause. If there is a causal link (e.g., simulation pre-commitment), model changes.\n\nNeed maybe discuss \"imaging\": Lewis proposed moving probability mass to closest A-worlds. Equation: P_im(A□→O)=Σ_w P(w) 1_{O at closest A-world to w} or probabilistic: Σ_w P(w) P_w^A(O). Need mention regress: need beliefs under certainty of A. Source says requires knowing what we would believe if certain of A; conditional to which assign probability <1 leading regress. Need mention that SCM is one modern way to specify imaging via structural equations, but still requires causal assumptions.\n\nNeed ensure not to claim things unsupported if source not mention Pearl. Clearly mark: \"The source article does not supply this algorithm; the following is a natural operationalization I add.\" Good.\n\nNeed maybe include mathematical example King David with variables and table? Could use equations.\n\nLet's design exact essay.\n\nTitle: Causal Decision Theory: A Working Mathematical Guide\n\nParagraphs:\n\nIntro:\n- rational choice under uncertainty; two expected utilities. Source: CDT selects action causing best outcome in expectation; EDT selects action that would be good news. Mention CDT prominent. Need about 150 words.\n\nSection 1: Evidential baseline.\n- Define decision problem: acts a ∈ A; outcomes o ∈ O; utility D(o); probability P over worlds. EDT formula: V_EDT(a)=Σ_o P(o | a)D(o). It treats choosing a as evidence. Example bad apple? maybe brief. Need maybe include apple/orange: eating apple causes happiness if good, sadness if bad. If uncertain, expected causal effects depend on prior good/bad, utilities. This is from source informal. Could include: if P(good)=0.8, D(happy)=10, D(sad)=-5; expected = 0.8*10+0.2*(-5)=7 for apple; if orange certain sad -2, choose apple. But CDT vs EDT difference when correlations. Maybe simple.\n\nSection 2: Causal expected utility with counterfactuals.\n- Gibbard/Harper formula: U(A)=Σ_j P(A→O_j)D(O_j). Need maybe use box arrow □→ to avoid material conditional. P(A □→ O_j) is probability assigned to counterfactual \"if A were performed, O_j would obtain.\" Not conditional P(O_j|A). Lewis proved probabilities of conditionals not generally equal conditional probabilities; if equal CDT=EDT. Mention independence condition. Source says Gibbard/Harper axioms and statistical independence of A and A->O_j sufficient; not always. Need explain in words.\n\nSection 3: Operationalizing with causal models (own proposal).\n- Because source says CDT doesn't specify algorithm. I propose SCM/do-operator. Define variables X, action A, outcome O, structural equations X_i=f_i(PA_i,U_i), exogenous distribution. Intervention do(A=a): replace equation A=f_A(...) with A=a. Then counterfactual probability P(A=a □→ O=o) = P_M(O=o | do(A=a)). Utility: U_CDT(a)=Σ_o P_M(o|do(a))D(o). If uncertain over models M_i with prior q_i: U(a)=Σ_i q_i Σ_o P_{M_i}(o|do(a))D(o). This is a recipe. Discuss policies: variable Π; do(Π=π). For dynamic, local CDT may evaluate immediate action; global policy evaluation requires intervention on policy variable; potential dynamic inconsistency if not bind future self. Need mention source leaves open; this is my interpretation.\n\nNeed maybe include \"screening off confounders\" example: common cause C. If A and O correlated via C, do(A) leaves C distribution unchanged: P(C|do(A))=P(C), not P(C|A). Then U_CDT differs from EDT. Good.\n\nSection 4: King David example.\n- Variables C ∈ {charismatic, uncharismatic}; A ∈ {send, abstain}; R revolt; utility. C fixed prior p. C causes just/unjust acts and revolt risk; A may be evidence of C but doesn't cause C. EDT uses P(C|A=send) high uncharismatic; may abstain. CDT uses P(C|do(send))=P(C); if sending has direct benefit and no causal effect on revolt except maybe through unjust act? Wait source says unjust acts themselves do not cause revolts; uncharismatic kings prone due bearing. So A=send is unjust but doesn't cause revolt; C causes both. Therefore CDT sees no causal path from send to revolt except maybe if sending causes something? It says sending for Bathsheba is unjust, but unjust acts don't cause revolts. So expected utility: U(send)=p*U(send,C)+... maybe direct pleasure minus no revolt change? If desire outweighs, send. Need formulate: If P(C)=prior, U(send)-U(abstain)=desire plus maybe no causal change in revolt. So CDT sends. Good.\n\nSection 5: Newcomb.\n- Variables P (prediction), B contents, choice T (one/two). Predictor sets B based on P. Causal assumption: choice occurs after content set, no backward causation. do(T=two) does not change B. Outcomes: if B=M, two M+1000 > one M; if B=0, two 1000 > one 0. Thus two-box dominates; U(two)=P(B=M)* (M+1000)+P(B=0)*1000; U(one)=P(B=M)*M + P(B=0)*0; difference=1000. EDT: P(B=M|one)≈.99, P(B=M|two)≈.01; one-box if .99M > .01M+1000? Yes. Mention source. CDT's answer hinges on causal model; if choice causally influences prediction (e.g., via predictor simulating decision process at time of choice), model differs. Source says at moment predictor already predicted; action won't affect outcome.\n\nSection 6: Dependencies and counterfactuals.\n- CDT evaluates relevant dependencies as counterfactual dependence under intervention, not mere statistical dependence. If O counterfactually depends on A through causal path, included. If A is evidence for O via common cause, not included unless intervention changes common cause? Need be precise: intervention on A may change descendants of A, not ancestors/common causes. Discuss confounding. In applying, ask: which variables would change if action were imposed by external manipulation? This is do. Need mention uncertainty about causal structure: average over dependency hypotheses; actions don't update dependency hypotheses unless action is evidence about them through a causal common cause? Hmm. Could say: If an action is correlated with a causal hypothesis because agents with certain dispositions choose it, CDT's standard formulation still holds the prior over hypotheses fixed when computing the act's causal value; this is the source of many objections. Need mark.\n\nSection 7: Vagueness, imaging, regress.\n- Source: CDT does not specify algorithm to calculate counterfactual probabilities. Lewis imaging: for each world w, move mass to closest A-world w_A; P(A□→O)=Σ_w P(w) I[w_A∈O]. Requires closest-world similarity and beliefs if certain of A; regress. SCM can be seen as specifying similarity/counterfactuals via equations, but needs causal assumptions. Source leaves open; my proposal: use explicit causal model or distribution over models. If no model, CDT underdetermined. Need mention principle of conditional excluded middle etc? Maybe briefly: Gibbard/Harper axioms plus independence can collapse to EDT; when not independent divergence.\n\nSection 8: Counterexamples.\n- Death in Damascus: Death predicts location; if you stay/flee, Death predicted. CDT sees moving doesn't causally change Death's prior location? Actually Death will go where predicted. If you choose to flee, if Death predicted flee, you die; if predicted stay, maybe survive? But due reliable prediction, conditional probability death high whichever. CDT may choose random? Source says CDT agent unable to process correlation and may make irrational decisions. Could explain: If Death's prediction is a common cause of your choice? Actually prediction based on your decision algorithm; your choice is correlated with prediction but not causing it. CDT's local intervention may not see benefit; if flee cost, may not flee; but if Death predicted stay, fleeing would save you. Since you don't know, maybe no action has positive causal expected value; randomization maybe. Critics say CDT unstable. Need keep brief.\n- Psychopath button: define variables Psychopath S, press A, outcome. CDT may press if prior probability psychopath low and causal benefit if not; but intuitive not press because pressing is strong evidence you are psychopath. Shows conflict between holding fixed prior vs evidential self-knowledge. Source claims Egan says pretty much everyone agrees not press but CDT endorses. Need note this depends on CDT formulation; some defenders may respond by including decision disposition in model. But source says Joyce argues CDT can take information about inclinations as evidence. Need mention: defenders say CDT can incorporate self-knowledge by treating inclinations as evidence about states, but critics argue standard calculation still fails. Need careful: source says Joyce argues CDT naturally capable of taking into account information about what one is inclined or likely to do as evidence. This is a defense. Could mention as open dispute.\n- Adversarial Offer: explain as money extraction. Use equations. Buyer can buy one or none. Seller predicts not acquire, puts $3. Prediction accuracy .75. CDT uses do(buy box) with prior probability box contains $3 maybe .75; expected value .75*3 -1 = 1.25 >0, buys. But if prediction tracks actual choice, ex post expected profit = .25*(3-1)+.75*(0-1)= -0.25, seller profits. This illustrates if action is correlated with predictor due to common disposition, CDT's holding fixed can be exploitable. Source says CDT requires buying one; profitable for seller. Need maybe mention Oesterheld/Conitzer Dutch books.\n\nSection 9: Practical recipe.\n- Steps: 1 specify acts/outcomes/utilities; 2 draw causal graph or dependency hypotheses; 3 identify action variable; 4 intervene (do), not condition; 5 compute outcome distribution; 6 take expectation; 7 for policies intervene on policy variable; 8 check dynamic consistency/precommitment; 9 if causal model uncertain, average over models with prior; 10 note if only correlations, CDT may ignore them. This helps unfamiliar cases.\n\nConclusion.\n\nNeed word count. Let's estimate: intro 150, baseline 250, counterfactual 300, operationalization 500, examples 600, dependencies/open 500, counterexamples 500, recipe 250, conclusion 100, references 100 = 3250. Need trim to under 3000. Maybe target 2700. Need concise. Could combine sections. Let's write with around 2600-2800. Need count approximate. Use headings. Ensure references not lengthy. Maybe in-text citations: (Wikipedia source), Gibbard & Harper, Lewis, Pearl? Since source asks target including references. Could include a short \"References\" list with 4 items. Word count includes references. Keep references ~80 words. Need maybe not include full bibliographic but enough. Source article, Gibbard & Harper, Lewis, Pearl maybe