(two)=p(M+1000)+(1-p)1000 = 1000 + pM.\nV(one)=pM+(1-p)0=pM.\nDifference 1000. Regardless of predictor accuracy p? Wait p is prior under do? If predictor accuracy, p maybe prior probability predicted one-box. Under do(two), contents distribution might equal prior probability predictor predicted one-box, not conditional on actual choice. If prior over predictor's prediction is maybe 0.5. Then difference 1000. Good.\nEDT values with accuracy .99:\nV_EDT(one)≈0.99M+0.01*0; V_EDT(two)≈0.01(M+1000)+0.99*1000. For M=1,000,000, one much larger. Good.\n\nNeed maybe use equations for adversarial offer:\nLet accuracy q=0.75. If CDT assigns P($3 in box i | do(buy i))=0.75? Actually source says CDT reasons at least one box contains $3; average box at least $1.50. How derive? If seller accurate .75, from buyer's perspective before choice, expected number of filled boxes = 2*0.75? Wait each box predicted not acquired with probability? Hmm. Source: \"CDT reasons that at least one box contains $3. Therefore, average box contains at least $1.50 in causal expected value.\" Maybe because seller accurate .75: if buyer chooses one box, the other box is not acquired, so if seller predicted correctly about other, it contains $3 with .75? Actually buyer may purchase one or none but not both. If buyer buys one, the other is not acquired. Seller predicted for each box whether buyer will acquire. Since she is accurate .75, at least one of the two predictions? Let's parse: Two boxes. Buyer can buy one or none, not both. Seller put $3 in each box she predicted buyer not to acquire. Prediction accurate with prob .75. From buyer's perspective, if they buy exactly one, for the unchosen box, seller's prediction \"not acquire\" is likely correct (prob .75), so unchosen box likely contains $3. But the chosen box? Seller predicted acquire with .75, so empty. CDT evaluating buying a box maybe sees causal effect of buying chosen box doesn't affect whether unchosen has money? Wait source says \"CDT reasons that at least one box contains $3. Therefore, the average box contains at least $1.50 in causal expected value\" Hmm maybe because if buyer may buy one of two, exactly one is not bought. The seller's accuracy 0.75 means expected number of boxes with $3 at least? Let's not over-analyze; can quote source: CDT sees average box at least $1.50, cost $1, buys. But if prediction is accurate about actual acquisition, buyer loses. Need present without deriving too exactly. But if I use equation .75*3-1 =1.25, source says average at least $1.50. Better align with source: CDT thinks each box has expected causal value at least $1.50? \"at least one box contains $3\" so average over two boxes at least $1.50. Since cost $1, buy one. But if seller's prediction accuracy .75, actual expected value of buying is negative. We can state source's reasoning rather than derive exact. Maybe include simple equation: if CDT assigns expected content ≥1.5, net ≥0.5 >0. Yet actual correlation makes purchase profitable for seller. Good.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"Dutch books\" briefly: Oesterheld & Conitzer argue CDT agents can be made to voluntarily lose money/diachronic Dutch books. Good.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"ratifiability\"? Not necessary. But maybe in dynamic inconsistency: CDT may choose action it later regrets? Source mentions Joyce regret and instability? Not in text except references. Could mention critics note instability. Keep brief.\n\nNeed ensure no instructions mention. Final should be essay.\n\nLet's draft mentally and monitor word count.\n\nPotential final:\n\n# Causal Decision Theory: Choosing by Expected Causal Consequences\n\nDecision theory ...\n\nUse Markdown headings.\n\nNeed approx 2400-3000 words. Let's draft with enough detail but concise.\n\nDetailed draft:\n\nTitle and intro (maybe 150 words)\n\n## 1. Two expected utilities\nDefine acts etc. Equations.\n\n## 2. Counterfactual expected utility\nGibbard-Harper equation; Lewis; independence.\n\n## 3. A working operationalization: interventions\nSCM, do, policies, uncertainty over models; mark own.\n\n## 4. Examples: David, Newcomb\nMaybe combine.\n\n## 5. What dependencies count?\nCommon causes, confounders, policy, dynamic.\n\n## 6. Open problems and counterexamples\nImaging regress, Death, psychopath, adversarial offer.\n\n## 7. Practical checklist\nSteps.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nReferences.\n\nNeed word count. Let's estimate each section:\nIntro 120\nTwo EU 260\nCounterfactual 260\nOperationalization 480\nExamples 500\nDependencies 300\nOpen/counterexamples 550\nChecklist 200\nConclusion 80\nRefs 100\nTotal ~2850. Good.\n\nNeed include mathematical definitions cleanly.\n\nLet's write final carefully. Need maybe use LaTeX notation. Need avoid overlong references. Need maybe cite source: \"The reference article summarizes CDT as...\" Good.\n\nPotential nuance: Source formula uses P(A → O_j) where arrow is counterfactual. In LaTeX maybe \\(P(A \\mathbin{\\square\\!\\to} O_j)\\). Could use \\(P(A \\counterfactual O_j)\\) but standard is \\(A \\boxright O\\). Use \\(A \\mathbin{\\triangleright} O\\)? Better: \\(P(A \\mathbin{\\square\\rightarrow} O_j)\\). Explain.\n\nNeed ensure mathematical correctness: In SCM, if outcomes are functions of exogenous variables, counterfactual probability after observing evidence can be more complex. For choice ex ante, interventional distribution is enough. Could mention \"for an agent who has not yet acted, the decision-relevant counterfactual probability is the interventional probability.\" Good.\n\nNeed mention \"counterfactual probability of it being the case that A necessarily implies O_j\" maybe source says necessarily. I can write \"probability that the counterfactual conditional 'if A were done, O_j would result' is true.\" Good.\n\nNeed discuss \"if conditional probabilities equal counterfactual probabilities\". Equation: If \\(P(A \\square\\rightarrow O)=P(O|A)\\) for all O, CDT=EDT. Lewis showed not generally. Gibbard/Harper: under axioms and independence, equality. Good.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"principle of conditional excluded middle\" not important but source says. Could say: \"with additional logical axioms, including a controversial conditional excluded middle\". Good.\n\nNeed maybe include \"dependency hypothesis\" formula:\nLet \\(H\\) denote a causal dependency hypothesis. Then\n\\[V_{\\mathrm{CDT}}(a)=\\sum_h P(H=h)\\sum_o P_h(O=o\\mid do(a))D(o).\\]\nThe source's discussion of Egan suggests CDT holds \\(P(H)\\) fixed rather than using \\(P(H|a)\\). Need state as my formalization. Good.\n\nNeed maybe include \"policy\" formula:\n\\[V_{\\mathrm{CDT}}(\\pi)=\\sum_o P(O=o\\mid do(\\Pi=\\pi))D(o).\\]\nNeed define \\(\\Pi\\). Good.\n\nNeed maybe include \"local action\" formula: At each decision node, choose action maximizing immediate causal EU given current info, but if policy requires future non-myopic choices, precommitment. Good.\n\nPotential issue: In sequential decision problems, standard CDT can evaluate policies by intervention on policy variable, but some philosophers debate whether CDT is dynamic inconsistent. Need mention source leaves open; my operational interpretation. Good.\n\nNeed maybe discuss \"news\" and \"management\". Good.\n\nLet's think of possible edge cases: If action has zero prior probability, conditional probability undefined; CDT can still evaluate via counterfactuals. This is a benefit. Could mention? Useful. In EDT, P(O|A) may be undefined or unstable for acts agent believes impossible; CDT with interventions can evaluate. Maybe include in formal section. Not necessary but good.\n\nNeed maybe discuss \"causal decision theory recommends actions causing best expected outcome\" but if action itself changes outcome only through evidence? Example: smiling makes people like you? That's causal. Good.\n\nNeed maybe discuss \"holding fixed initial views about likely causal structure\" from Egan. Use quote-like. Good.\n\nNeed maybe mention \"source says philosophers usually prefer CDT when required to choose between CDT and EDT.\" Could include. But not necessary. Could mention to contextualize.\n\nNeed maybe include \"apple/orange\" informal. Use in first section. Let's craft:\n\"If eating an apple causes happiness and orange causes sadness, choose apple. If apple may be good/bad, expected causal effect = P(good)D(happy)+P(bad)D(sad).\" This from source. Good.\n\nNeed maybe include exact numbers? Could keep simple. Maybe no numbers to save words. But examples help. Use small numbers maybe. \"If P(good)=0.8, happy=10, sad=-5, expected apple=7; if orange=-2, choose apple.\" Good but word count. Fine.\n\nNeed maybe note \"desirability D\" not probability; utility.\n\nNeed think about references and licensing. Since source is Wikipedia CC BY-SA, if we adapt maybe need attribution. The user supplied source; we can include reference. We can state \"This essay draws on the Wikipedia article...\" Good.\n\nNeed final conclusion brief.\n\nNeed ensure no analysis in final. Final only essay.\n\nLet's begin writing final. Need keep word count. I'll produce around 2700 words. I can roughly count? Let's write and approximate. Maybe use word counter mentally? We'll try.\n\nDraft:\n\n# Causal Decision Theory: Choosing by Expected Causal Consequences\n\nIntro: 130\n\n## 1. Expected utility, evidentially understood\n...\n\nLet's draft fully:\n\n[Will write final in my head then output.]\n\nNeed be careful with LaTeX syntax. Use \\( ... \\). Good.\n\nPotential final text:\n\n\"Causal decision theory (CDT) is a school of thought within decision theory holding that a rational agent should choose the action that causes the best outcome in expectation. The reference article contrasts this with evidential decision theory (EDT), which chooses the action that would be the best news if the agent learned it had been taken.\" Good.\n\nThen formal.\n\nNeed maybe include \"The source article's central formula is...\" Good.\n\nLet's write section 1:\n\n\"Let \\(\\mathcal A\\) be a set of available acts, \\(\\mathcal O\\) outcomes, and \\(D:\\mathcal O\\to\\mathbb R\\) desirability. A probability function \\(P\\) represents the agent's credences. EDT evaluates\n\\[V_{EDT}(a)=\\sum_o P(O=o\\mid A=a)D(o).\\]\nThis is conditional expectation: it asks what outcome should be expected given the news that \\(a\\) was performed. If eating an apple is likely to cause happiness... But conditional probabilities can encode merely evidential connections.\" Good.\n\nSection 2:\n\n\"Gibbard and Harper... \\(U(A)=\\sum_j P(A \\square\\rightarrow O_j)D(O_j)\\). The symbol ... probability of counterfactual. If outcomes exhaustive, etc. Lewis proved ... not equal. If equal, CDT collapses to EDT. Gibbard/Harper showed under axioms + independence equality; but actions/conditionals not independent in cases like King David.\" Good.\n\nNeed maybe define \\(A\\) as proposition event? Fine.\n\nSection 3 operationalization:\n\n\"The source says CDT does not itself specify an algorithm for computing \\(P(A\\square\\rightarrow O)\\). I will therefore add a standard modern operationalization...\" Good.\n\nDefine SCM:\n\"A structural causal model \\(M\\) has variables \\(V\\), equations \\(X_i=f_i(PA_i,U_i)\\), exogenous distribution. For action variable \\(A\\), intervention \\(do(A=a)\\) replaces equation for A. Then \\(P_M(O=o\\mid do(A=a))\\) is the probability that outcome would occur if A were imposed. CDT value ... If multiple models h with prior p(h), ...\" Good.\n\nPolicy:\n\"To evaluate policy \\(\\pi\\), introduce policy variable \\(\\Pi\\) and set \\(do(\\Pi=\\pi)\\), changing all action equations according to π. This is my extension; source focuses on single acts. In sequential settings, evaluating adoption of policy may differ from evaluating each future act locally; precommitment can change causal structure.\" Good.\n\nSection 4 examples:\n\nKing David:\n\"David wants Bathsheba; fears revolt. Charisma C fixed. C causes revolt risk and just/unjust acts; sending is unjust but doesn't cause revolt. EDT: P(C=uncharismatic | send) high, so revolt expected, abstain. CDT: do(send) leaves distribution of C unchanged; no causal path from send to revolt; if direct utility of Bathsheba positive, send. Mathematically, \\(P(C\\mid do(send))=P(C)\\), whereas EDT uses \\(P(C\\mid send)\\).\" Good.\n\nNewcomb:\n\"Predictor sets box B before choice. Let B=M or 0. CDT assumes no causal arrow from choice to B. do(two) leaves B distribution fixed. Values... difference 1000; two-box. EDT uses conditional probabilities, one-box.\" Good.\n\nSection 5 dependencies:\n\n\"CDT counts dependencies that survive intervention. If O changes when A is externally set, relevant. Correlations due to common cause C are not relevant unless A causally influences C or C is downstream. The practical question: Which variables would change if the act were imposed? In dependency hypothesis notation... CDT uses prior over hypotheses. This is what Egan criticizes: holding fixed unconditional credences.\" Good.\n\nSection 6 vagueness/counterexamples:\n\n\"Source notes vagueness; Lewis imaging... regress. SCM addresses but requires causal model. Counterexamples: Death in Damascus, psychopath button, adversarial offer.\" Need detail.\n\nDeath: \"Death predicts location; if reliable, your choice is correlated with prediction. CDT may not see fleeing as causally improving because Death's prediction already fixed; critics say this is irrational.\" Maybe mention if Death prediction is perfect, every act has bad conditional evidence; CDT may be indifferent or randomize, but source says unable to process correlation. Good.\n\nPsychopath: \"Paul... CDT may press if prior psychopath low, because pressing causes killing psychopaths only if not psychopath; but pressing is evidence he is psychopath. Intuitive not press. CDT's holding fixed psychopathy yields press according to Egan.\" Need be precise: If button kills all psychopaths, and Paul may be one, causal effect of pressing includes death if psychopath. Under do(press), psychopathy fixed. Expected utility = P(psychopath)*D(death)+P(not)*D(no psychopaths). If P(psychopath) very small and D(no psychopaths) high, press. EDT uses P(psychopath|press) high, not press. Good.\n\nAdversarial offer: \"Two boxes cost $1; seller put $3 in boxes predicted not acquired; accuracy .75. Source says CDT reasons at least one contains $3, average box ≥$1.50, so buy. But if prediction tracks buyer's choice, buying tends to select empty box; seller profits. This supports claim CDT can voluntarily lose money and be Dutch-booked.\" Good.\n\nDefender response: \"Joyce says CDT can take information about inclinations as evidence; disputes remain. Source leaves whether these are fatal open.\" Good.\n\nSection 7 checklist:\n\n\"For unfamiliar case: 1 list actions/outcomes/utility; 2 specify causal graph/dependency hypotheses; 3 identify action variable; 4 replace conditioning with intervention; 5 compute distribution; 6 average over uncertainty; 7 for policies intervene on policy variable; 8 check if act provides only news; 9 consider precommitment/dynamic consistency.\" Good.\n\nConclusion.\n\nReferences:\n- Wikipedia contributors, Causal decision theory, revision 1381555528, 2026.\n- Gibbard & Harper 1981.\n- Lewis 1981 maybe 1976? Need mention Lewis 1976 probabilities conditionals. Could include both? Keep short: Lewis 1976; Lewis 1981. Maybe 3 refs plus Pearl. But lengthy? We'll list 4.\n\nNeed maybe not include full details but enough.\n\nNow, need ensure final word count. Let's approximate after writing. We'll output. But maybe need more mathematical precision in policy and dependencies. Good.\n\nLet's write final now with polished prose. Ensure approx 2400-3000. I can maybe include around 2500. Need avoid too many examples? The user asks use examples whenever helpful. Good.\n\nLet's draft final fully.\n\nPotential final:\n\n(Start)\n\n# Causal Decision Theory: Expected Utility as Causal Expect